   "source": [
    "# Telco Customer Churn Prediction"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 1. Problem Definition & Dataset Selection"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "**Objective:** To analyze the factors influencing customer churn and to build a predictive model that can identify customers who are likely to churn. This is a classification problem.\n",
    "**Dataset:** `WA_Fn-UseC_-Telco-Customer-Churn.csv`. This dataset contains customer information and their churn status."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 2. Data Cleaning & Preparation"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 1,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "# Import necessary libraries for data analysis and visualization\n",
    "import pandas as pd\n",
    "import numpy as np\n",
    "import matplotlib.pyplot as plt\n",
    "import seaborn as sns\n",
    "\n",
    "# Set the style for our plots to make them look nice\n",
    "sns.set(style=\"whitegrid\")"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 2,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "# Load the dataset from the CSV file into a pandas DataFrame\n",
    "df = pd.read_csv('WA_Fn-UseC_-Telco-Customer-Churn.csv')\n",
    "\n",
    "# Let's take a look at the first few rows of our data to see what it looks like\n",
    "df.head()"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### 2.1 Handle Missing Values"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 3,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "# We need to check if there are any missing values in our dataset.\n",
    "# The 'TotalCharges' column has missing values. Let's find out how many.\n",
    "df['TotalCharges'] = pd.to_numeric(df['TotalCharges'], errors='coerce')\n",
    "print(f'Number of missing values in TotalCharges: {df[\"TotalCharges\"]}.isnull().sum()\n')\n",
    "\n",
    "# Since the number of missing values is small compared to the total number of rows, we can safely drop them.\n",
    "df.dropna(inplace=True)\n",
    "\n",
    "# Let's check again to make sure the missing values are gone.\n",
    "print(f\"Number of missing values in TotalCharges after cleaning: {df['TotalCharges'].isnull().sum()}\n")"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### 2.2 Remove Duplicates"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 4,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "# Now, let's check for any duplicate rows in our data.\n",
    "print(f'Number of duplicate rows: {df.duplicated().sum()}\n')\n",
    "\n",
    "# If there were duplicates, we would remove them using df.drop_duplicates(inplace=True)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### 2.3 Handle Outliers"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 5,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "# We'll look for outliers in the numerical columns: 'tenure', 'MonthlyCharges', and 'TotalCharges'.\n",
    "# A good way to spot outliers is to use boxplots.\n",
    "plt.figure(figsize=(15, 5))\n",
    "\n",
    "plt.subplot(1, 3, 1)\n",
    "sns.boxplot(x=df['tenure'])\n",
    "plt.title('Boxplot of Tenure')\n",
    "\n",
    "plt.subplot(1, 3, 2)\n",
    "sns.boxplot(x=df['MonthlyCharges'])\n",
    "plt.title('Boxplot of Monthly Charges')\n",
    "\n",
    "plt.subplot(1, 3, 3)\n",
    "sns.boxplot(x=df['TotalCharges'])\n",
    "plt.title('Boxplot of Total Charges')\n",
    "\n",
    "plt.show()"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "The boxplots show that there are no significant outliers that need to be handled."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### 2.4 Encode Categorical Data"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 6,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "# Our model can only understand numbers, so we need to convert our categorical columns into numerical ones.\n",
    "# We'll use one-hot encoding for this.\n",
    "\n",
    "# Create a copy of the dataframe to work with\n",
    "df_encoded = df.copy()\n",
    "\n",
    "# Encode the 'Churn' column to 1 for 'Yes' and 0 for 'No'\n",
    "df_encoded['Churn'] = df_encoded['Churn'].apply(lambda x: 1 if x == 'Yes' else 0)\n",
    "\n",
    "# Identify categorical columns for one-hot encoding, excluding 'customerID'\n",
    "categorical_cols = df_encoded.select_dtypes(include=['object']).columns.tolist()\n",
    "categorical_cols.remove('customerID')\n",
    "\n",
    "# Perform one-hot encoding\n",
    "df_encoded = pd.get_dummies(df_encoded, columns=categorical_cols, drop_first=True)\n",
    "\n",
    "# Display the first few rows of the encoded dataframe\n",
    "df_encoded.head()"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### 2.5 Normalize/Scale Data"
   ]
  }
